<title>BRAZIL: Brazil official eyes $16 bln in investment in '97.</title>
<headline>Brazil official eyes $16 bln in investment in '97.</headline>
<dateline>SAO PAULO 1997-01-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil should attract $16 billion in direct foreign investment this year, nearly twice as much as in 1996, Minister of Trade and Industry Francisco Dornelles told Jornal do Brasil.</p>
<p>Most of the money would go toward developing Brazil's telecommunications, petroleum and hydroelectric sectors and improving its ports, Dornelles said.</p>
<p>He told the newspaper that the level of investment in Brazil would grow to between 17 percent and 18 percent of gross domestic product this year from 16 percent of gdp in 1996.</p>
<p>This year's anticipated privatization of giant mining concern Companhia Vale do Rio Doce (CVRD) is expected to fetch $8.0 billion alone.</p>
<p>--John Miller, Sao Paulo newsroom, 5511 248-5414</p>
